2. The majority opinion refers to Swanson’s inclusion of “largely extraneous facts” in her complaint. Why does the majority think they were extraneous? Does the dissent agree that they are extraneous? If they are extraneous, why did Swanson include them in the complaint? If you were her attorney, which of these facts would you have included?

BEYOND THE DOCTRINE:
THE IMPACT OF TWIQBAL
Few cases have generated the controversy and predictions of dramatic effects that Twombly and Iqbal have. It is easy to see why: pleading is an essential part of every civil action, and the Supreme Court, after decades of stability in pleading doctrine and practice, injected a new uncertainty and a new concept—“plausibility pleading”—into the doctrine. Would Twiqbal lead to a rise in cases being terminated after a motion to dismiss? Would Twiqbal deter plaintiffs from filing actions in federal court? Would categories of plaintiffs most in need of discovery to uncover wrongdoing by defendants, such as employment discrimination plaintiffs (and civil rights plaintiffs more generally), see their cases routinely dismissed?

